
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I put a design together — the event vocabulary, which is the interface everything else hangs off.

  [1mThe fork:[0m which kinds of change are worth telling a watcher about.

  Two are ruled out by earlier decisions, so I'm not offering them: [1mtask created[0m can't notify anyone (you can't watch a
  task that doesn't exist yet), and [1mcomments[0m would need a whole comment model invented to serve the fiction rather than
  the notifications.

  - [1mStatus + edit + delete[0m — "Alice marked X done", "Alice renamed X", "Alice deleted X". Buys: three distinct message
    shapes, so the dropdown looks like a real feed instead of one sentence repeated. Delete also forces the interesting
    case: a notification whose task no longer exists. Costs: three event types to handle, and that dangling-reference
    case to get right.
  - [1mStatus changes only[0m — just done/not-done. Buys: the smallest possible event set, one message shape. Costs: the feed
    is monotonous, and it dodges the dangling-reference problem that any real system hits.
  - [1mEverything the model has[0m — status, every field edit, delete, and watch/unwatch activity. Buys: most complete. Costs:
    watch activity isn't a task change, so it muddies what the feed means.

  The event type is stored on each notification record, so [1madding a type later is cheap[0m; the one thing worth deciding
  now is whether deletion is in scope, because that's the case that shapes how notifications reference tasks.

  [1mMy recommendation: status + edit + delete.[0m It's the smallest set that produces a feed worth looking at, and including
  delete now means the reference model is right from the start rather than retrofitted.
